the day of the crash is seared in Polish  history one of those occasions when  every poll remembers where they were the  Polish Air Force plane came down in  Smolensk in Russia it claimed not only  the lives of all 96 passengers and crew  on board but a country's political and  military elite amongst the dead the  Polish President Lech Kaczynski  and his wife Maria eight years on new  questions are being asked about what  caused the tu-154 aircraft to come down  in his first television interview a  British Air Accident investigator has  told Sky News he believes the original  Polish and Russian findings of pilot  error are wrong I think they did not do  a thorough investigation they probably  it seems to be that they had come to  that conclusion very very early on and  then possibly sought evidence to  justified Russia released this audio  which it says capture the last few  moments of the flight Russian  investigators say in dreadful weather  conditions the pilot flying too low hit  a birch tree searing the wing roof Frank  Taylor was involved in the Lockerbie and  Manchester air crash investigations  he's now examined high-resolution  photographs taken by the Russians of the  Smolensk crash if the tip had been cut  off by a birch tree that the damage  would wouldn't have looked like this  well it would have been it would have  been bent back consistently backwards  rather than up down and maybe even  forward Frank Taylor says he believes  damage to the left side cabin door is  consistent with what he calls massive in  tonal pressure leading him to this  conclusion there is no doubt that there  were explosions onboard before the  aircraft hit hit the ground relatives  have long complained that Russia failed  to protect the crash site pointing to  unverifiable video such as this Russia  has refused to return any wreckage or  the full flight data recordings  political activist Ana valentinovich was  on the plane in Poland her son says he  was threatened with jail for speaking  out after the wrong body was returned to  him and that no relative can be sure  they have actually buried their loved  ones those on board were on their way to  the 70th commemoration of the Katyn  Massacre of 22,000 polish intellectuals  politicians and military officers by the  Soviets in World War two a member of the  Federation of khatyn families spoke to  us on behalf of the smelling skeletons  but wanted to remain anonymous over  fears for his personal safety this is no  longer a Polish problem this has become  a European problem and perhaps an  international problem there now needs to  be a cohesive effort to deal with both  the air accident investigation side and  the human rights violations have clearly  occurred there are some important  questions to ask Russia most of the  relatives are utterly traumatized  they've been waiting for eight years to  discover the truth what level of  intimidation have they experienced over  their quest to find the truth some  relatives have reported being threatened  and abused by hooligans I think it would  be fair to describe it as they have  lived in a climate of fear Russia says  the crash has already been fully  investigated friends and relatives of  those who died say an EU resolution  calling for an independent inquiry has  been ignored even so they won't give up  on their belief that the full story of  the Polish Air Force flight is still to  be revealed Lisa Holland Sky News
 

